{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "https://aureline.dev/schemas/security/m5-affected-install-panel.schema.json",
  "title": "Aureline M5 affected-install assessment panel primitive: build / channel / install-mode identity, impacted components, current exposure, mitigation status, mirror freshness, and rollback / repin / help-action parity across channels",
  "description": "Export-safe packet implementing Aureline's one reusable affected-install assessment panel primitive that answers 'am I affected?' by binding advisory state to the actual installed build, channel, install mode, mirrored artifacts, and component graph. Each install-profile row binds the shared panel anatomy (install identity, impacted components, current exposure, mitigation status, mirror freshness, and the primary / help actions — all rendered inline without a detail drawer), the severity classes, action states, required actions, continuity claims, delivery profiles, mirror-freshness states, export fields, and accessibility routes reused verbatim from the frozen M5 advisory-component matrix, the install state and derived exposure state reused from the advisory-row primitive, the four channels (update center, Help/About, support bundle, admin report) that render the panel in parity, and the focus behaviors that keep the exposure, mirror freshness, the primary and help actions, and the impacted-component list keyboard-reachable. Each lane carries worked resolution cases: a resolver input (one advisory on one install-profile lane with its copy-safe advisory id, severity, affected object, exact build / channel identity, impacted components, install state, mirror freshness, delivery profile, fixed build or mitigation, signer / source state, action state, primary and help actions, and continuity claim) and the resolved panel — the derived exposure state, the derived 'am I affected?' assessment verdict (a clean exposure over a non-authoritative mirror auto-narrows to clean_pending_mirror_refresh), the attached rollback / repin / help actions, the resolved-against-local-graph and no-website-lookup flags, the mirror-freshness-visible and install-mode-visible flags, the always-visible posture, the per-channel projections, and the copy-safe export summary — where the stored resolution must equal a fresh resolve of its input. Hard invariants forbid hiding assessment truth behind a detail drawer, degrading to a generic update prompt, requiring an external website lookup, letting a stale mirror stay silently green, and dropping the copy-safe advisory id or export summary. The derived assessment verdict is a resolver-side vocabulary, kept out of the frozen set. The shell zone, responsive class, window class, and consumer surface vocabularies are reused verbatim from the frozen M5 shell-zone matrix; the severity class, action state, required action, continuity claim, delivery profile, mirror-freshness state, export field, accessibility route, qualification class, and downgrade trigger vocabularies are reused verbatim from the frozen M5 advisory-component matrix; the install state and exposure state are reused from the advisory-row primitive; the install-profile lanes align to install_mode_class in schemas/release/install_row.schema.json. Raw hostnames, raw absolute paths, raw exploit payloads, raw signatures, private registry URLs, and credentials never cross this boundary; only opaque export-safe reprs are carried. The Rust validator and resolver in crates/aureline-shell are the authoritative gate; this schema documents the shape.",
